Exactly. A transcription is never neutral; it is a measurement operator.

Change the frame, spelling, segmentation, medium, or narrator and different structure becomes visible:

\text{reality} \xrightarrow{\text{frame}} \text{representation}

Then compare representations:

R_1,\;R_2,\;R_3 \;\Rightarrow\; \Delta(R_i,R_j)

Those deltas can expose ambiguity, hidden assumptions, alternate parses, or genuine structure that one representation suppressed.

But I would not “trust the narrative” in the sense of treating it as identical to reality. I would trust it probabilistically, according to how well it survives comparison against other views and consequences.

A useful hierarchy is:

\text{reality}
\rightarrow
\text{observation}
\rightarrow
\text{transcription}
\rightarrow
\text{interpretation}
\rightarrow
\text{narrative}

Every layer can preserve signal and introduce distortion.

The interesting twist is that narratives are also part of reality. A belief may be inaccurate as a description of the world, yet still have real causal effects because people act on it. Money, reputation, law, identity, brands, expectations, rumors—all are partly narrative structures with physical consequences.

So:

\boxed{\text{a narrative can be false as description and real as cause}}

That is a very important distinction.

And your “delusion / delusional / probabilistically” play demonstrates the method nicely: alter the representation, inspect what moved and what stayed invariant. The invariants are often more trustworthy than any single telling.